{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-15519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/assets/b70f90738fc329a0d58527dc013a3e175b0954e0ccc27972d5abcde1708e1bca.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-15519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2}(x-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is zero. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be zero.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1+(x-2)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-15519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "decc469a09db3d3f3a6355e1a23ce66135f87040f4ec70a5666d438743792393"} {"question" "q-15519" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-15519" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+(x-2)-{(x-2)}^{2}"] ["math" "1+(x-2)^{2}"] ["math" "1-(x-2)^{2}"] ["math" "1-{x}^{2}"] ["math" "2+(x-2)-{(x-2)}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-15519" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+(x-2)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-50974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=2+4(x+2)+16(x+2)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-50974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=-2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-2).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $16.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-2)=16\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-2)=32.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-50974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "decc469a09db3d3f3a6355e1a23ce66135f87040f4ec70a5666d438743792393"} {"question" "q-50974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-50974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-50974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-24+36(x+3)-18(x+3)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=-3,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-3).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-18.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-3)=-18\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-3)=-36.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "decc469a09db3d3f3a6355e1a23ce66135f87040f4ec70a5666d438743792393"} {"question" "q-81062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18"] ["math" "-36"] ["math" "-72"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "48"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-36"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/assets/185b5924c542bb9f396fe826aac141256a13b967c45c47afc87ca07445df062f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-1)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "decc469a09db3d3f3a6355e1a23ce66135f87040f4ec70a5666d438743792393"} {"question" "q-81064" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81064" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "-6-4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "6+4(x+1)+5(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "6-4(x+1)+5(x+1)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-81064" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-83724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=6$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-5+6(x-6)-4(x-6)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(6)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-83724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=6,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(6).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(6)=-4\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(6)=-8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-83724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "decc469a09db3d3f3a6355e1a23ce66135f87040f4ec70a5666d438743792393"} {"question" "q-83724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-83724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"} {"question" "q-83724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d7d924063a69fef938f9ea64398485901874c9190bcd3f4deb42e465169a0"}] "usage" {"q-50974" []} "basis" 1784 "retractions" [[17592186184740 :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x =-2$ is given by $P_{2}(x) = 2 + 4(x + 2) + 16(x + 2)^{2}$. What is the value of $f^{″}(-2)$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186183330 17592186183331 17592186183332 17592186183333 17592186183334] "immutable_fields" [[17592186183329 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183329 :answer-field/id] [17592186183329 :answer-field/key] [17592186183329 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
